x86_64-linux  Three differences, all of them the virtual-base feature's own remainder and none of them this file's subject. (1) The **VTT and the construction vtables**: clang emits `_ZTT1Q`, `_ZTT2D1`, `_ZTT1R` and `_ZTC1R0_2D1`, which exist so a virtual call made from *inside* a base constructor reaches that level's overrider. cxx1 emits none and refuses that shape instead of emitting a table it has not measured - the same remainder virtual-base-member.nonames records. (2) **`_ZTI1R` and `_ZTS1R`**: clang gives R a type_info because a class with a virtual base is dynamic on this ABI, where cxx1 emits the vtable without one; R has no virtual function, so nothing here reads it, but `typeid(R)` would not link. (3) cxx1 emits `_ZN1RC2Ev` and `_ZN1QC2Ev` where clang emits neither - the base-subobject constructors, which nothing in this file calls because nothing derives from R or Q. An unused definition is not a wrong one, and the split into C1/C2 is what makes "a virtual base is built once" work at all; virtual-base-diamond measures it.
arm64-darwin  The same three with Mach-O's leading underscore.
x86_64-windows  Two, and they are one gap seen from both sides: **the Microsoft RTTI for a class with a virtual base**. clang emits `??_R1A@73FA@V@@8`, the base-class descriptor for V inside Q's hierarchy descriptor, whose numbers carry where the vbptr is and which entry of the vbtable reaches V. cxx1 emits no such descriptor and instead emits `??_R4V@@6B@`, a complete-object locator for V - which V, having no virtual function of its own, has no use for. `dynamic_cast` and `typeid` across a virtual base are what read these, and the encoding is its own measurement on the box. **The vbtable this file is about matches cl exactly** - `??_8D1@@7B@ 0, 16`, `??_8R@@7B@ 0, 24`, `??_8Q@@7B@ -8, 16` - as do all four sizes; see the header of the .cpp.
